{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The path of life upward, for the discerning, to turn away from Sheol beneath (Proverbs 15:24).</b>" "<b>Solomon,</b> peace be upon him, informs us here (Proverbs 15) that the discerning man who recognizes the nature of this world — that it is a world of loss — knows that there is a path of life upward in the supernal, eternal world; and therefore he removes and withdraws himself from the lusts of this world and does not make of it a permanent abode — this is 'to turn away from Sheol beneath.' The parable for this is a man dwelling in a certain city who knows that his dwelling there is not permanent but temporary, for his intention and thought is to go to another city where he is destined to dwell permanently. There is no doubt that he will not exert himself to acquire there fields and vineyards, nor utensils and household goods, since his intention is not to fix his dwelling and settle there. So too the discerning man who knows there is a path of life upward does not exert himself in bodily matters at all in this world, except in what is necessary, and in those things that are a preparation for the service of the Holy One, blessed be He, without which he cannot endure — for he knows that the supernal world is a permanent abode and this world is only a temporary sojourn. And since this world is vanity and emptiness, and it is not fitting for the discerning to make of it a foundation except in the fear of HaShem, blessed be He, therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, began his book (Ecclesiastes 1:2): 'vanity of vanities,' and concluded with the attribute of fear. Or the meaning of the verse may be: 'The path of life upward, for the discerning' — that it speaks to the discerning man and says to him: You, the discerning one, know that you have a path of life upward, and for this 'turn away from Sheol beneath,' that is, you must withdraw from bodily lusts in order to merit the path of life which is above." "<b>According to</b> this interpretation, the word ״סור״ ('turn away') is an imperative, not an infinitive, and the verse is an admonition and command to the discerning man: if he wishes to merit eternal life, let him turn away from bodily pleasures, which are 'Sheol beneath.' And the reason it used the term ארח ('path') rather than דרך ('way') is that it is from the language of ארח ('wayfarer'), for man in this world is like a sojourner in the land and like a wayfarer lodging for the night. And just as the wayfarer who enters an inn knows that his departure is tomorrow and his dwelling there is brief, and he yearns to return to his place and the land of his birth, so the discerning man considers himself a sojourner and wayfarer in this world, and he knows and grasps that above there is a path of life, and his soul yearns to return to its root, which is the source of life. Therefore the soul is called חיה ('living'), and indeed it is living by nature. And so the philosophers have said: the soul is living by nature, dead by accident — and the accident is sin; and the body is dead by nature, living by accident — for its vitality is only by virtue of the conjunction of soul and body, and separation is the death of the body." "<b>And it is known</b> that the death of the body is the cause of life for the soul. Of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 7:1): 'and the day of death [is better] than the day of one's birth' — praising the day of death over the day of birth, both in this world and in the world to come. In the world to come, it is because the soul cannot attain the supernal human stature except through the death of the body; and in this world, it is because a man's praises and good deeds are not recognized and made known except on the day of his death — for on the day of birth no one knows who he is, but on the day of death all recognize him and recall the good deeds and righteous acts that he did. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded concerning Sarah our mother: all the days that Sarah lived, she had a lamp burning from Sabbath eve to Sabbath eve, and a blessing found in the dough, and a cloud bound over the tent; and when she died, these ceased, and her merit became publicized. Moreover, she died in the land of Israel and was buried in the land of Israel after having lived long — as it is written: 'And the life of Sarah was a hundred years, and twenty years, and seven years — the years of the life of Sarah.' And written immediately after it: 'And Sarah died in Kiriath-arba — the same is Hebron — in the land of Canaan.' This parashah was fitting to be joined and placed immediately above, right after the parashah of the Binding (akedah), for upon hearing the report of the Binding, Sarah died and her soul flew away." "<b>And from</b> what is well known: all the parashot in the Torah, just as they are arranged and juxtaposed one to the next, there is great reason for their juxtaposition, for they were not joined without purpose. Rather, from the juxtaposition of parashot it will sometimes become clear to us a glorious wisdom — whether in demonstrating the renewal of the world, or in the secret of formation, as in this parashah; or in the wisdom of nature, as in the juxtaposition of the parashah of plagues (negaim) to the parashah of 'a woman when she conceives' (Leviticus 12); and so too in other wisdoms. And the reason that the parashah of Nahor came here and interrupted in between is to say that at the time of the Binding, immediately after those generations, Rebekah the mate of Isaac was born; and when Rebekah was born, Sarah had already died. Therefore he juxtaposed immediately: 'And Sarah died.'" "<b>And from this point on.</b> It has become clear to us the juxtaposition of Rebekah in the narrative, both to the portion of the Binding [of Isaac] that precedes it and to the portion of Sarah's death that follows it. And what they said in the Midrash, that therefore the death of Sarah was juxtaposed to the portion of the Binding — the Midrash made Rebekah and Sarah as though they were one, because Rebekah was not born until Sarah died, and because she was in her place and also she resembled her in her good deeds, as they expounded: ״שרה אמו״ — 'Sarah his mother' — ״והנה היא שרה אמו״ — 'and behold, she is Sarah his mother'; and this is the Midrash that I mentioned." "<b>(1) A hundred years and twenty years and seven years.</b> The verse should have said 'a hundred and twenty and seven years.'" "<b>But</b> by way of the plain sense (peshat), it is the way of Scripture to mention 'years' for each and every item, and the reason is to distinguish between the parts of the number, which are three: units, tens, hundreds. And so you find with Abraham (Genesis 25:7): ״מאת שנה ושבעים שנה וחמש שנים״, and likewise with Ishmael it says (ibid.): ״מאה שנה ושלשים שנה ושבע שנים״. 'The years of Sarah's life' — all of them were equal in merit." "<b>And</b> by way of the Midrash: 'A hundred years and twenty years and seven years' — at a hundred she was like a twenty-year-old regarding sin, and at twenty she was like a seven-year-old regarding beauty. And its explanation is that her beauty continued to increase at age twenty, which is the time of standing [at full stature], like a seven-year-old, which is the time of increase — for you already know that a person's lifespan has three parts: childhood, youth, and old age. Childhood is the time of increase and growth; youth is the time of standing at one constitution; old age is the time of diminution and decline." "<b>Furthermore,</b> by way of the Midrash: 'the years of Sarah's life' is like the numerical value of ויהי״ו ל״״ז, for from the day Isaac was born until the day of the Binding was thirty-seven years, and they were the essence of Sarah's life — for before that she had no son, and whoever has no children is accounted as dead. Therefore it says ״ויהיו חיי שרה״, because the essence of her life was like the numerical value of ויהי״ו. But 'the years of Sarah's life' — that is, all the years she lived were one hundred and twenty-seven years. Similarly (Genesis 47:28): ״ויחי יעקב בארץ מצרים שבע עשרה שנה״ — when Joseph was separated from him, seventeen years, and seventeen years in Egypt, making thirty-four, the numerical value of ויח״י, for those were the essence of Jacob's life, and everything in between was a life of suffering." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say further: ״ויהיו חיי שרה״ — 'and the life of Sarah was' — is said concerning the life of the World to Come. And the reason for this is that you find in the sequence of the parashah the report of the Akedah and that her soul flew forth, and therefore Scripture says ויהיו, which is an expression of permanence, and does not say שנה (year), for those lives do not fall under time and are not compounded of years. שני חיי שרה — 'a hundred years of the life of Sarah' — is the life of this world, and therefore it is mentioned by number and Scripture says in it שנה, for it is the number compounded of years."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:45.093072024Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}